   $q$On a coordinate plane, Line A passes through $(0, -3)$ and $(1, -1)$. Line B passes through $(0, 9)$ and $(9, 0)$. At what point do the two lines intersect?$q$, $q$[{"text":"$(4, 5)$","is_correct":true},{"text":"$(8, 1)$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"slope-as-run-over-rise"},{"text":"$(5, 4)$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"swapped-coordinates"},{"text":"$(12, 21)$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"moved-term-without-changing-sign"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$$(4, 5)$$q$, $q$Line A rises $2$ for each $1$ to the right, so $y = 2x - 3$. Line B falls $9$ over a run of $9$, so $y = -x + 9$. Set equal: $2x - 3 = -x + 9$, so $3x = 12$ and $x = 4$. Then $y = 2(4) - 3 = 5$, giving $(4, 5)$. The pair $(8, 1)$ uses a slope of $\frac{1}{2}$ for Line A, which is run over rise.$q$, 'draft', null)
