   $q$For what value of $k$ does the system $3x - 2y = 5$ and $6x - 4y = k$ have infinitely many solutions?$q$, $q$[{"text":"$k = -10$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"sign-error-when-scaling"},{"text":"$k = 5$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"did-not-scale-constant"},{"text":"$k = \\frac{5}{2}$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"divided-instead-of-multiplied"},{"text":"$k = 10$","is_correct":true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$$k = 10$$q$, $q$The left side $6x - 4y$ is exactly $2$ times $3x - 2y$. For the equations to describe the same line, the right side must also be doubled: $k = 2 \times 5 = 10$. The choice $k = 5$ doubles the left side but not the constant, which gives parallel lines and no solution.$q$, 'draft', null)
