  ('93270436-0dd4-5161-b713-37bd6d0b30d1', null, (select id from public.skills where slug = 'systems-linear'), null, 'medium',
   $q$Line P passes through $(-2, 0)$ and $(0, 4)$. Line Q passes through $(0, -2)$ and $(3, 4)$. How many solutions does the system formed by these two lines have?$q$, $q$[{"text":"Exactly two solutions","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"counted-lines-as-solutions"},{"text":"Exactly one solution","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"assumed-different-lines-intersect"},{"text":"Infinitely many solutions","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"compared-slopes-only"},{"text":"No solution","is_correct":true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$No solution$q$, $q$Line P has slope $\frac{4 - 0}{0 - (-2)} = 2$ and $y$-intercept $4$, so $y = 2x + 4$. Line Q has slope $\frac{4 - (-2)}{3 - 0} = 2$ and $y$-intercept $-2$, so $y = 2x - 2$. Equal slopes with different intercepts mean parallel lines, so there is no solution. Matching slopes alone does not mean the lines are the same; the intercepts must match too.$q$, 'draft', null)
