   $q$If $3x + 2y = 4$ and $5x - 3y = 13$, what is the value of $x + y$?$q$, $q$[{"text":"$1$","is_correct":true},{"text":"$3$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"sign-error-solving-for-y"},{"text":"$-1$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"reported-y-only"},{"text":"$2$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"reported-x-only"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$$1$$q$, $q$Multiply the first equation by $3$ and the second by $2$: $9x + 6y = 12$ and $10x - 6y = 26$. Adding gives $19x = 38$, so $x = 2$. Then $3(2) + 2y = 4$ gives $2y = -2$ and $y = -1$. So $x + y = 2 + (-1) = 1$. The value $3$ comes from taking $y = 1$ instead of $-1$.$q$, 'draft', null)
