   $q$A proof of the Vertical Angles Theorem uses two intersecting lines. $\angle 1$ and $\angle 2$ form a linear pair, and $\angle 2$ and $\angle 3$ form a linear pair, so $\angle 1$ and $\angle 3$ are vertical angles. Step 1: $m\angle 1 + m\angle 2 = 180^\circ$. Step 2: $m\angle 2 + m\angle 3 = 180^\circ$. Step 3: $m\angle 1 + m\angle 2 = m\angle 2 + m\angle 3$. Step 4 uses the Subtraction Property of Equality. Which statement completes Step 4?$q$, $q$[{"text":"$m\\angle 1 + m\\angle 3 = 180^\\circ$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"assumed-vertical-angles-supplementary"},{"text":"$m\\angle 1 = m\\angle 2$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"confused-adjacent-with-vertical"},{"text":"$m\\angle 2 = 90^\\circ$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"assumed-the-lines-are-perpendicular"},{"text":"$m\\angle 1 = m\\angle 3$","is_correct":true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$$m\angle 1 = m\angle 3$$q$, $q$Subtracting $m\angle 2$ from both sides of Step 3 leaves $m\angle 1 = m\angle 3$, which is what the theorem claims. Nothing in the proof says the lines are perpendicular, so $m\angle 2 = 90^\circ$ is not justified, and $\angle 1$ and $\angle 2$ are adjacent, not vertical.$q$, 'draft', null)
