   $q$A cube with edge length $6$ cm can be cut into three congruent pyramids. All three pyramids share one corner of the cube as their apex, and each has as its base one of the three cube faces that do not touch that corner. What is the volume of one pyramid, in cubic centimeters?$q$, $q$[{"text":"$216$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"gave-cube-volume"},{"text":"$36$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"divided-by-six"},{"text":"$108$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"split-into-two"},{"text":"$72$","is_correct":true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$$72$$q$, $q$The cube's volume is $6^3 = 216$, and three congruent pyramids fill it exactly, so each has volume $\frac{216}{3} = 72$. This matches $\frac{1}{3} \cdot 36 \cdot 6 = 72$ and shows where the one third in the pyramid formula comes from. The value $36$ divides by the six faces, but only three pyramids are made.$q$, 'draft', null)
