   $q$A guy wire runs in a straight line from the top of an $18$-meter vertical pole to a stake in the level ground. The wire makes a $62^\circ$ angle with the ground. How long is the wire, to the nearest tenth of a meter?$q$, $q$[{"text":"$20.4$ meters","is_correct":true},{"text":"$38.3$ meters","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"used-cosine-for-opposite-side"},{"text":"$9.6$ meters","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"found-ground-distance-instead-of-wire"},{"text":"$15.9$ meters","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"multiplied-by-sine-instead-of-dividing"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$$20.4$ meters$q$, $q$The pole height $18$ is opposite the $62^\circ$ angle and the wire is the hypotenuse, so $\sin 62^\circ = \frac{18}{L}$ and $L = \frac{18}{\sin 62^\circ} \approx 20.4$ meters. The value $15.9$ meters is shorter than the pole itself, which is impossible for the hypotenuse.$q$, 'draft', null)
