  ('2d44f888-bdbb-5642-8673-e5f1d598722c', null, (select id from public.skills where slug = 'similarity-right-triangles'), null, 'medium',
   $q$A straight mountain road climbs at a steady $4^\circ$ angle above the horizontal. A cyclist rides $2.5$ miles along the road. Using $1$ mile $= 5280$ feet, how many feet higher is the cyclist at the end, to the nearest foot?$q$, $q$[{"text":"$923$ feet","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"used-tangent-with-hypotenuse"},{"text":"$921$ feet","is_correct":true},{"text":"$0.17$ feet","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"forgot-to-convert-miles-to-feet"},{"text":"$13168$ feet","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"used-cosine-for-opposite-side"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$$921$ feet$q$, $q$The road distance is the hypotenuse: $2.5 \times 5280 = 13200$ feet. The rise is opposite the $4^\circ$ angle, so it is $13200\sin 4^\circ \approx 921$ feet. The value $923$ feet uses tangent, which would need the horizontal distance rather than the distance along the road.$q$, 'draft', null)
