   $q$From the top of a $60$-meter lighthouse, a keeper sees two boats due east, both on the same side of the lighthouse and in line with its base. The angles of depression to the boats are $35^\circ$ and $20^\circ$. How far apart are the boats, to the nearest tenth of a meter?$q$, $q$[{"text":"$20.2$ meters","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"multiplied-by-tangent-instead-of-dividing"},{"text":"$250.5$ meters","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"added-distances-for-same-side-boats"},{"text":"$79.2$ meters","is_correct":true},{"text":"$223.9$ meters","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"used-difference-of-angles"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$$79.2$ meters$q$, $q$Each angle of depression equals the angle of elevation from that boat. The horizontal distances are $\frac{60}{\tan 20^\circ} \approx 164.85$ meters and $\frac{60}{\tan 35^\circ} \approx 85.69$ meters. The boats are on the same side, so subtract: $164.85 - 85.69 \approx 79.2$ meters. The value $250.5$ meters adds the distances, which would only fit boats on opposite sides.$q$, 'draft', null)
