   $q$Let $P(x) = x^2 + 1$ and $Q(x) = x$. Which of these results is NOT a polynomial?$q$, $q$[{"text":"$P(x) + Q(x)$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"thinks-sums-can-leave-polynomials"},{"text":"$P(x) - Q(x)$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"thinks-differences-can-leave-polynomials"},{"text":"$P(x) \\div Q(x)$","is_correct":true},{"text":"$P(x) \\cdot Q(x)$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"thinks-products-can-leave-polynomials"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$$P(x) \div Q(x)$$q$, $q$The sum $x^2 + x + 1$, the difference $x^2 - x + 1$ and the product $x^3 + x$ are all polynomials, because polynomials are closed under those operations. The quotient is $\frac{x^2 + 1}{x} = x + \frac{1}{x}$, and $\frac{1}{x}$ is a negative power of $x$, so it is not a polynomial. Polynomials are not closed under division.$q$, 'draft', null)
