   $q$An arithmetic sequence has explicit formula $a_n = 5 + 3(n-1)$ for $n \geq 1$. Which recursive definition gives the same sequence?$q$, $q$[{"text":"$a_1 = 5$, $a_n = 3a_{n-1}$ for $n \\geq 2$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"treated-arithmetic-as-geometric"},{"text":"$a_1 = 5$, $a_n = a_{n-1} + 3$ for $n \\geq 2$","is_correct":true},{"text":"$a_1 = 3$, $a_n = a_{n-1} + 5$ for $n \\geq 2$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"swapped-first-term-and-difference"},{"text":"$a_1 = 2$, $a_n = a_{n-1} + 3$ for $n \\geq 2$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"used-constant-of-simplified-form-as-first-term"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$$a_1 = 5$, $a_n = a_{n-1} + 3$ for $n \geq 2$$q$, $q$When $n = 1$, $a_1 = 5 + 0 = 5$, and each increase in $n$ adds $3$. So $a_1 = 5$ and $a_n = a_{n-1} + 3$. The definition starting at $a_1 = 2$ uses the constant from $3n + 2$, but $2$ would be the term for $n = 0$, which is not in this sequence.$q$, 'draft', null)
