   $q$Which infinite geometric series has a finite sum?$q$, $q$[{"text":"$4 + 4 + 4 + 4 + \\dots$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"thought-ratio-of-one-converges"},{"text":"$9 - 3 + 1 - \\frac{1}{3} + \\dots$","is_correct":true},{"text":"$5 + 10 + 20 + 40 + \\dots$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"ignored-size-of-ratio"},{"text":"$2 - 4 + 8 - 16 + \\dots$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"thought-alternating-signs-converge"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$$9 - 3 + 1 - \frac{1}{3} + \dots$$q$, $q$An infinite geometric series has a finite sum only when $|r| < 1$. For $9 - 3 + 1 - \dots$, $r = -\frac{1}{3}$ and $|r| = \frac{1}{3} < 1$, so it converges (to $\frac{9}{1 + \frac{1}{3}} = \frac{27}{4}$). The series $2 - 4 + 8 - \dots$ alternates in sign, but $|r| = 2$, so its terms grow and it has no finite sum.$q$, 'draft', null)
