  ('3c42d1b6-fa70-5dbe-ab06-15bf35a8456d', null, (select id from public.skills where slug = 'trigonometric-functions'), null, 'hard',
   $q$A function of the form $y = a\sin(bx) + d$, with $a > 0$, $b > 0$ and $x$ in radians, has a maximum value of $9$, a minimum value of $-3$ and a period of $\pi$. Which equation fits?$q$, $q$[{"text":"$y = 6\\sin(2x) + 3$","is_correct":true},{"text":"$y = 12\\sin(2x) + 3$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"used-full-range-as-amplitude"},{"text":"$y = 6\\sin(2x) + 6$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"added-max-and-distance-of-min"},{"text":"$y = 6\\sin\\left(\\frac{1}{2}x\\right) + 3$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"inverted-b-from-the-period"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$$y = 6\sin(2x) + 3$$q$, $q$The amplitude is half the distance from max to min: $\frac{9 - (-3)}{2} = 6$. The midline is the average: $\frac{9 + (-3)}{2} = 3$. The period is $\frac{2\pi}{b} = \pi$, so $b = 2$. That gives $y = 6\sin(2x) + 3$. The choice $y = 12\sin(2x) + 3$ uses the whole distance $12$ as the amplitude.$q$, 'draft', null)
