  ('e7840bb5-5c33-5d46-bb6e-a45269824ff7', null, (select id from public.skills where slug = 'trigonometric-functions'), null, 'hard',
   $q$An angle $\theta$ in Quadrant IV has $\sin\theta = -\frac{12}{13}$. What is $\tan\theta$?$q$, $q$[{"text":"$-\\frac{12}{13}$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"gave-sine-instead-of-tangent"},{"text":"$-\\frac{5}{12}$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"inverted-the-tangent-ratio"},{"text":"$\\frac{12}{5}$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"ignored-quadrant-sign"},{"text":"$-\\frac{12}{5}$","is_correct":true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$$-\frac{12}{5}$$q$, $q$Use $\sin^2\theta + \cos^2\theta = 1$: $\cos^2\theta = 1 - \frac{144}{169} = \frac{25}{169}$, and cosine is positive in Quadrant IV, so $\cos\theta = \frac{5}{13}$. Then $\tan\theta = \frac{\sin\theta}{\cos\theta} = \frac{-12/13}{5/13} = -\frac{12}{5}$. The choice $-\frac{5}{12}$ divides cosine by sine instead.$q$, 'draft', null)
