   $q$The angles are in radians. What is the exact value of $\sin\frac{4\pi}{3} + \cos\frac{11\pi}{6}$?$q$, $q$[{"text":"$\\sqrt{3}$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"wrong-sign-on-sine-in-quadrant-iii"},{"text":"$0$","is_correct":true},{"text":"$-\\sqrt{3}$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"wrong-sign-on-cosine-in-quadrant-iv"},{"text":"$\\frac{\\sqrt{3} - 1}{2}$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"used-cosine-value-for-the-sine-term"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$$0$$q$, $q$$\frac{4\pi}{3}$ is in Quadrant III with reference angle $\frac{\pi}{3}$, so $\sin\frac{4\pi}{3} = -\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}$. $\frac{11\pi}{6}$ is in Quadrant IV with reference angle $\frac{\pi}{6}$, so $\cos\frac{11\pi}{6} = \frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}$. The sum is $-\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2} + \frac{\sqrt{3}}{2} = 0$. The choice $-\sqrt{3}$ makes the cosine negative, but cosine is positive in Quadrant IV.$q$, 'draft', null)
